  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 259 PM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday...\n\nMoving into the weekend, the pattern aloft will become more \namplified as a shortwave trough axis drops southeast from the \nMidwest into the Ohio Valley and may attempt to close off. \nAssociated with this upper level wave, a surface wave of low \npressure will advance toward the region in tandem with the upper \nwave.  \n\nAhead of this wave, a cluster of showers/storms looks likely for \nlate Friday night and Saturday morning within a warm \nadvection/nocturnal low-level jet regime.  Many questions remain on \nthe longevity of this morning convection and its potential to limit \ninstability/diurnal heating Saturday afternoon ahead of an \napproaching cold frontal boundary.  In places that heating does \noccur, dewpoints will be in the low-mid 70s which will be ample for \nconvection.  Model proximity soundings show relatively weak shear \nacross the region during the day with values of less than 30 knots. \nHowever, there remains an emerging signal that some low-level wind \nenhancement could develop over our region Saturday afternoon and \nevening.  Should this higher shear come to fruition, along with more \nrobust heating, a risk of severe weather will be on the table. Based \non model soundings, damaging winds would be the primary hazard, \nthough isolated tornadoes could also occur.  Additionally, a fairly \nhigh surge of moisture will be drawn up into the Ohio Valley ahead \nof the approaching front.  Model soundings show PWATs increasing to \nover 2 inches across the area.  So heavy/torrential rainfall will \nlikely accompany any thunderstorms and flash flooding will also be a \nthreat.\n\nBy Sunday, the upper trough will likely lag behind across the Ohio \nValley while the surface low and cold front push on off to the east. \nSome lingering shower activity seems rather likely here along with \nplentiful cloud cover which will keep temps in the upper 70s/lower \n80s.\n\nMoving into the new work week, stronger flow in the westerlies will \nretreat back to the north along the US/Canadian border while more \npersistent ridging will hold sway over the western US.  Across the \neastern US, the latest data suggests that a baggy trough axis will \nremain in place from southern New England back down into the SE US. \nUnder this scenario, it seems that showery weather may continue \nthrough about midweek, with showers/storms focused mainly in the \nafternoon/evenings.  Daytime highs will be near normal with mid-\nupper 80s for highs and overnight lows in the mid-upper 60s.",
